# Drafted by Gemini (R14 renathyr), edited.

~ cathedral
quest the_priors known
Narrator: Red light from the tall glass spills across the altar stone, and the smell of old incense hangs heavy over cold iron.
father_renathyr [smile]: The Twilight bell has rung, travelers, and the sanctuary is quiet. Easy, Duchess, they are guests. Come forward and warm yourselves by our grace.
* Your fires keep the valley pure, Father. -> cath_praise
* Tell us of the Crimson Rose. -> cath_goddess
* What became of Gareth Creed? -> cath_gareth
* You carry yourself like a knight. What were you before? -> cath_past
* We will leave you to your prayers. -> cath_end

~ cath_praise
father_renathyr [smile]: A clear eye sees the mercy in the ash. Only what is pruned may grow straight, my friends.
set crimson_devotions += 1
-> cathedral

~ cath_goddess
father_renathyr [neutral]: The Crimson Rose is a mother to those left out in the frost. She asks only that we bleed away our pride and keep her garden clean.
-> cathedral

~ cath_gareth
if flag.gareth_fate == "rescued"
father_renathyr [weary]: A brother has been spared the pyre, and I call that a mercy. Be still, Duchess, the debt is paid.
else
father_renathyr [sad]: Master Creed was offered the open hand of the faithful, and he chose the ash instead. I wept for him when the smoke rose.
endif
-> cathedral

~ cath_past
father_renathyr [neutral]: Old iron rusts when you leave it in the rain, and old titles with it. Speak to my three priors if you wish to learn the order of our house.
-> cathedral

~ cath_end
father_renathyr [smile]: Rest beneath our roof tonight, and let the bells keep watch for you. Peace be upon your steps.
set cathedral_audience_done
set audience_stamp = now
-> END

~ belfry_talk
Narrator: Frost rims the belfry rail high above the valley, while the bats stir and click among the great silver bells.
father_renathyr [weary]: The Witching Hour is the honest hour, when even the choir sleeps. Quiet, Duchess, there is no foe on the stair.
* What are you watching down in the mist? -> belfry_mist
* Did you know a woman named Lethica? -> belfry_lethica
* [Insight DC 13] He knows the goddess is a devil. -> belfry_tell | belfry_blind
* Your reign of blood ends tonight, Marius. -> belfry_fight
* We will leave you to the night air. -> belfry_end

~ belfry_mist
father_renathyr [sad]: Far across Moonsong Lake, you can almost catch the lanterns of Wickermoor. I kept a lamp in a high window once, waiting on the dawn.
-> belfry_talk

~ belfry_lethica
Narrator: His gauntleted fingers tighten on the pommel of his blade until the leather groans.
father_renathyr [weary]: Lethica. Keep that name out of the cold air, travelers, for the mountain has no room for ghosts.
set renathyr_past_hint
-> belfry_talk

~ belfry_tell
Narrator: His eyes catch the red glass below, hollow and clear, like a man looking through his own bars.
father_renathyr [weary]: A drowning man does not ask what reaches down into the black water, friend. Once you have drunk the thorny cup to the dregs, there is no riding home.
-> belfry_talk

~ belfry_blind
Narrator: His face stays as still as carved altar stone, giving nothing back to the wind.
father_renathyr [neutral]: A shepherd keeps his vigil because the wolves do not sleep, nothing more.
-> belfry_talk

~ belfry_fight
Narrator: He strikes the rim of the nearest bell with his hilt, and the bronze note shivers down through the stone.
father_renathyr [angry]: Wake, Duchess, and remember our oath! If you come with steel in my own tower, the Rose will have your blood upon the roof!
set belfry_talk_done
combat mon_belfry_fight

~ belfry_end
father_renathyr [weary]: Go down to the warm halls, travelers. The high wind is no place for the living.
set belfry_talk_done
-> END
